Edward Nelson (1932–2014) was an influential American mathematician known for his groundbreaking work in mathematical physics, probability theory, and logic, including the development of Internal Set Theory and stochastic mechanics.

Early Life and Education
Edward Nelson was born on May 4, 1932, in Decatur, Georgia. He spent part of his early childhood in Rome, Italy, while his father worked with the Italian YMCA under Benito Mussolini's regime. During World War II, he relocated with his mother to New York City, attending Bronx High School of Science. He later returned to Italy for high school at the Liceo Scientifico Giovanni Verga. Nelson earned his M.S. in 1953 and Ph.D. in 1955 from the University of Chicago under Irving Segal, with a dissertation on Markov processes

Academic Career
Following his postdoctoral tenure as a National Science Foundation fellow at the Institute for Advanced Study (1956–1959), Nelson joined the mathematics faculty at Princeton University in 1959, becoming a full professor in 1964 and serving until retirement in 2013. Throughout his career, he mentored numerous students and was noted for fostering independent, innovative research in his advisees 

Major Contributions
Constructive Quantum Field Theory: Nelson pioneered the use of probability in quantum field theory, providing rigorous methods for interacting fields and influencing the Euclidean approach in mathematical physics. His seminal works include proofs of two-dimensional interacting scalar field models and contributions to renormalization techniques 

Stochastic Mechanics: In the 1960s, he introduced stochastic mechanics as a probabilistic interpretation of quantum mechanics, modeling particle motion via Brownian-like stochastic processes. This framework derived the Schrödinger equation from classical mechanics combined with stochastic dynamics 

Mathematical Logic and Foundations: Nelson developed Internal Set Theory (IST), an extension of conventional set theory that formalizes nonstandard analysis, enabling rigorous incorporation of infinitesimals. He also explored ultrafinitism, the consistency of arithmetic, and predicative arithmetic, challenging established notions within set theory 

Graph Theory: Edward Nelson formulated what is now known as the Hadwiger–Nelson problem, asking for the chromatic number of the plane such that no two points a unit distance apart share the same color 

Honors and Legacy
Nelson's contributions earned him the 1995 Leroy P. Steele Prize for Seminal Contributions to Research from the American Mathematical Society. He was elected to the American Academy of Arts and Sciences (1975), the National Academy of Sciences (1997), and became a Fellow of the American Association for the Advancement of Science (2003). His groundbreaking work in probability, quantum mechanics, and logic left a lasting influence on mathematics and inspired generations of mathematicians 

Personal Life
Nelson was described as a radical nonconformist, deeply curious and courageous in his pursuit of mathematical truth 
. He was married twice, to Nancy Wong Nelson and Sarah Jones Nelson, and had two children. He passed away on September 10, 2014, in Princeton, New Jersey, due to complications from lymphoma 
. Edward Nelson remains celebrated for his innovative approach to mathematics, blending rigorous logic, probability, and creativity, and for inspiring others to approach problems with independence and insight.
